Minor crush injury without obvious fracture from fingers caught in drawer / car door to distal phalanges
Minor Trauma and Personal Injury
Throbbing fingertip pain post-compression, Intact DIP joint mobility, Small non-disruptive subungual hematoma

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: MTI-014
Reason for Visit: Acute severe fingertip pain and swelling after a crush injury, requiring evaluation for fracture and nailbed laceration.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage reported a high-force crush injury to the digit with severe pain and nail discoloration. Urgent clinical evaluation was necessary to assess for distal tuft fractures, perform neurovascular checks, and decide if immediate nail trephination was required before morning clinical availability.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: I slammed my finger in the car door and the tip is throbbing intensely and turning black under the nail.
History of Present Illness: Injury sustained via a sudden compression mechanism. Patient reports acute, throbbing, intense localized pain in the distal phalanx immediately following the event. A visible subungual hematoma developed rapidly, covering approximately 30 percent of the nail bed area. There is mild to moderate tense swelling of the distal fingertip pulp. The patient retains normal active and passive range of motion at the distal interphalangeal joint. The pain is worst at the fingertip pad and nail bed.
Functional Status: Impaired fine motor dexterity due to localized pain.
Social/Safety Concerns: Minor temporary restriction in typing or fine manual tasks.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 130/80 | HR: 86 | Temp: 36.6 | RR: 16 | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 78 Kg
General: Alert, nursing the injured hand, holding it elevated above the heart.
Physical Exam: Digit examination reveals mild tense swelling of the distal fingertip pulp. There is throbbing fingertip pain post-compression. A small, non-disruptive subungual hematoma (less than 50% nail area) is present. The nail fold is intact, and the nail plate is stable without avulsion or deep laceration. Intact DIP joint mobility is confirmed. Two-point discrimination and distal capillary refill remain intact. There is an absence of gross clinical deformity, abnormal angulation, or rotational malalignment. Tenderness is generalized over the tuft rather than a sharp, specific linear bone pain. Intact flexor and extensor tendon function.
Environmental Observations: Heavy door identified as the mechanical cause; no ongoing hazard.
Medication Reconciliation: No routine medications.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Minor crush injury to distal phalanx - Status: Stable
Differentials:

Distal tuft fracture - Ruled out due to: Absence of severe pinpoint bony tenderness, lack of severe angulation, and intact stable nail plate.

Nail bed laceration - Ruled out due to: Intact nail plate without structural disruption and subungual hematoma covering <50% of the area.

Tendon rupture (Mallet/Jersey finger) - Ruled out due to: Intact active flexion and extension against resistance at the DIP joint.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The mechanism of a crush injury to the fingertip carries a high risk for complex open fractures and severe nailbed disruption. On-site assessment safely excluded severe structural deformity, confirming isolated soft tissue crush injury and a minor subungual hematoma. The urgent after-hours evaluation was critical to rule out open fractures requiring antibiotics, verify intact neurovascular status, and confirm that painful nail trephination was clinically unwarranted, safely managing the patient without requiring an emergency department visit.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Conservative management initiated. Applied a simple protective dressing. Advised oral paracetamol and ibuprofen. Plain radiographs are not immediately required but should be obtained if pain worsens over 48 hours.
Positive Behavioral Change: Keep the injured hand strictly elevated above heart level on a pillow while resting. This harnesses gravity to drain the throbbing fluid away from the tight fingertip space, dramatically reducing pain and throbbing overnight.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None.
DME/Safety (Optional): Tubular finger bandage for protection.
Follow-up: Routine GP review in 3 to 5 days to monitor the subungual hematoma and ensure the nail plate remains stable.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Present to the Emergency Department if the entire finger becomes numb, pale, and cold, or if the fingernail begins to lift entirely off the nailbed with severe bleeding.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Occult tuft fractures can sometimes present with identical symptoms; if pain does not improve, an x-ray is necessary.
Clinical Course: The acute throbbing will diminish significantly over 48-72 hours. The black blood spot under the nail will harmlessly grow out over the next 4 to 6 months.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Seek emergency medical attention if your fingertip turns completely white and stays cold, or if pus begins to leak from around the edges of the nail.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: Elevation relies on gravity to decrease the arterial blood pounding into the inflamed, tight fingertip space.
Actionable Steps: Sleep with your hand elevated on two pillows. Do not attempt to pierce the nail yourself to drain the blood. Protect the tip from minor bumps.

